          "text": "Refine @TODO.md based on the following:\nAdvanced Algorithmic and Hardware Accelerations for the Aho-Corasick Automaton: Strategies for High-Throughput Linguistic Pattern Matching1. Introduction and Architectural ContextThe evolution of multi-pattern string matching algorithms has been fundamentally driven by the escalating need to process vast streams of data in real-time. From deep packet inspection (DPI) and network intrusion detection systems (NIDS) to natural language processing (NLP) and sophisticated grammatical linting engines, the ability to locate hundreds or thousands of specific string patterns concurrently within an input text is an absolute cornerstone of modern computational text analytics. Among the various algorithmic strategies designed to solve this complex computational problem, the Aho-Corasick (AC) algorithm, originally introduced in 1975 by Alfred V. Aho and Margaret J. Corasick at Bell Laboratories, remains the most resilient and foundational paradigm. By constructing a specialized finite-state automaton capable of processing input data streams in a single, continuous pass, the classical Aho-Corasick algorithm guarantees a linear time complexity relative to the length of the input text. This theoretical efficiency ensures that the algorithm operates in mathematical time bounds that are entirely independent of the size or complexity of the underlying pattern dictionary.However, theoretical algorithmic efficiency often collides with the physical limitations of modern computer hardware. As the scale of pattern dictionaries grows from a few dozen keywords to tens of thousands of complex structural rules, classical implementations of the Aho-Corasick algorithm encounter severe hardware-level bottlenecks. The memory footprint required to store the massive state transition tables grows exponentially, leading directly to cache thrashing, pipeline stalls, and catastrophic memory latency issues on contemporary Central Processing Unit