ional CPU architectures—particularly in fields such as massive-scale genomics sequencing and backbone network traffic analysis—academic researchers have successfully ported the Aho-Corasick algorithm to General Purpose Graphics Processing Units (GPGPUs) and Application-Specific Integrated Circuits (ASICs).6.1 Overcoming Thread Divergence with Parallel Failure-Less Aho-Corasick (PFAC)The primary and most debilitating challenge of executing the standard Aho-Corasick algorithm on a modern GPU is the phenomenon known as thread divergence. GPUs operate on a rigid Single Instruction Multiple Thread (SIMT) architecture, where hundreds or thousands of processing cores are forced to execute the exact identical instruction simultaneously on distinct pieces of localized data. If one specific thread encounters a successful character match and advances its state deeper into the trie, while an immediately neighboring thread simultaneously encounters a mismatch and jumps backwards via a failure link, the execution paths instantly diverge. The GPU hardware is physically incapable of processing these divergent instructions simultaneously. It must serialize these divergent paths, executing one branch while the other threads sit idle, severely degrading the overall parallel performance of the silicon.To explicitly mitigate the devastating effects of thread divergence, researchers introduced the Parallel Failure-Less Aho-Corasick (PFAC) algorithm. PFAC radically and permanently alters the internal topology of the automaton by systematically locating and removing every single failure transition from the state machine.Under the PFAC architecture, if a character mismatch occurs while traversing a pattern path, rather than dropping back via a complex failure link to a generalized suffix state, the PFAC automaton simply halts execution and abruptly terminates the thread's matching attempt. To ensure that no potentially overlapping multi-character matches are accidentally bypassed, the PFAC algorithm brute-forces the problem by launching a completely new, independent processing thread at every single byte offset of the entire input payload.Because there are absolutely no failure links, every active thread only ever moves forward down the trie structure. Consequently, the state transitions become perfectly predictable and entirely uniform across entire GPU warps (groups of 32 parallel threads on NVIDIA architectures). Furthermore, this heavily simplified state transition logic requires significantly fewer memory lookups per character, explicitly reducing complex data dependencies. Exhaustive experimental results published in IEEE proceedings demonstrate that deploying PFAC on a modern GPU can achieve an astonishing 4,000x speedup compared to standard sequential CPU-based Aho-Corasick implementations, while also logging a 3x throughput speedup over competing GPU-based string matching methodologies.6.2 Hardware Deflation via the Split-AC AlgorithmWhile GPUs are masterful at handling massive, batched throughput, highly constrained embedded systems, smart network interface cards (Smart NICs), and hardware firewalls must rely on Field Programmable Gate Arrays (FPGAs). In these specialized environments, embedded block RAM (BRAM) is strictly limited and extremely precious.The Split-AC algorithm is a structural architectural optimization custom-tailored for hardware compilation. Instead of attempting to compile 10,000 network signatures into a massive, unmanageable monolithic matrix that would overflow the physical BRAM, Split-AC exploits domain-specific characteristics to physically divide the global automaton into multiple, localized, smaller state machines. These smaller, compartmentalized machines operate entirely in parallel over the incoming data stream. Because each of the smaller machines possesses vastly reduced alphabet coverage and significantly shorter state depths, they can be subjected to extreme compression algorithms, ultimately requiring 28% to