nclusions and SynthesisThe pursuit of accelerating the Aho-Corasick algorithm has evolved dramatically beyond the classical, mathematically elegant, yet memory-inefficient sparse matrix DFAs initially developed in the mid-1970s. As conclusively demonstrated through the rigorous synthesis of contemporary computational literature, hardware design documents, and open-source benchmarking reports, extracting maximum analytical throughput from multi-pattern string-matching algorithms requires a deeply integrated alignment with the microscopic realities of the underlying hardware architecture.For massive, enterprise-scale environments dealing exclusively with raw network packet inspection and signature matching, highly parallelized, failure-less GPU implementations (such as PFAC) or advanced, shift-or based SIMD architectures (such as FDR) provide the requisite multi-gigabit throughput to keep pace with backbone internet traffic. These approaches prioritize parallel mathematical execution over complex state traversal.However, for sophisticated software applications firmly embedded within nuanced linguistic linting ecosystems—such as the Rust-based zhtw-mcp server operating on complex, multi-byte Traditional Chinese payloads—the most profound, actionable acceleration resides strictly within the realm of aggressive memory compression and optimal CPU cache locality. By systematically transitioning the core string-matching engine from a traditional pointer-based NFA representation to a highly compacted Double-Array Trie (DAT) architecture via advanced open-source crates like daachorse, software systems can immediately secure a 56% to 60% permanent reduction in automaton memory footprint. This spatial victory runs concurrently alongside a 3.0x to 5.2x absolute increase in raw character traversal speeds due to minimized L1 cache misses.Furthermore, by structurally adapting the resulting automaton to natively process char-wise Unicode scalars rather than raw UTF-8 byte streams, t