            "new_string": "### 45.2 Specialized rule filter fast paths\n- [ ] Per-match filtering after AC hit runs a cascade of conditional\n      stages: exclusion check, superstring guard, word-boundary check,\n      context-clue AC scan, positional-clue search, exception check,\n      severity computation.  Each rule uses a different subset.\n  - Inspired by regex VM instruction sets (Cox, MoonBit, Odin): the\n    principle of compiling rule-specific execution paths at build time\n    to eliminate dead branches at match time.\n  - Current state: the code already skips major work via precomputed\n    flags (`spelling_has_superstring`, optional clue IDs, profile/type\n    gates).  A full bytecode VM risks adding indirection overhead that\n    negates branch-prediction savings, especially on short filter chains.\n  - Preferred approach (simpler alternatives first):\n    1. Precomputed bitflags per rule indicating which filter stages are\n       active.  Single `u8` bitmask, checked with `&` before each stage.\n    2. Monomorphic fast paths for common rule classes: rules with no\n       clues, rules with clues but no positional, rules with both.\n       Three specialized `process_spelling_match_*` variants, dispatched\n       once per rule class at AC build time.\n    3. Only if (1) and (2) prove insufficient: bytecode micro-VM with\n       `FilterOp` enum (~7 opcodes including CHECK_EXCEPTION).\n  - Prerequisite: 44.1 (`tracing` instrumentation) must land first to\n    profile the filter cascade and confirm branch misprediction is\n    measurable.  If not, defer.\n  - Scope: `engine/scan/spelling.rs` (fast paths), optionally new\n    `engine/scan/filter_vm.rs` if approach (3) needed.\n  - Gate: identical scan output on full test suite.  No regression on\n    100KB benchmark.  Measurable improvement on rules with 4+ filter\n    stages.\n\n### 46.1 Build-time AC serialization (zero-copy startup)\n- [ ] Scanner::new() rebuilds the daachorse CharwiseDoubleArrayAhoCorasick\n      on every instantiation.  For a stateless MCP server handling per-call\n      invocations, this construction cost (~5-10ms for 1100+ patterns) is\n      paid on every request with no amortization.\n  - The double-array packing algorithm (finding collision-free slots in\n    BASE/CHECK arrays) is the expensive part; the resulting integer arrays\n    are static once built.  Shift this cost entirely to compile time.\n  - Phase 1 -- `build.rs` serialization:\n    1. Add `build.rs` that loads `assets/ruleset.json`, deduplicates\n       patterns, builds the charwise DAAC, and serializes the resulting\n       BASE/CHECK/FAIL/output arrays to a binary blob.\n    2. Embed the blob via `include_bytes!(\"../target/ac_spelling.bin\")`.\n    3. At runtime, deserialize with zero-copy (daachorse's\n       `DoubleArrayAhoCorasick::deserialize` or manual reconstruction\n       from raw integer slices).\n  - Phase 2 -- profile-aware variants:\n    If profile-gated rule filtering (strict_moe vs default) produces\n    meaningfully different pattern sets, build one serialized AC per\n    profile.  Otherwise, a single AC with post-match profile gating\n    (current approach) is simpler.\n  - Constraint: daachorse does not currently expose a serialize/\n    deserialize API.  Options: (a) upstream a PR, (b) fork with serde\n    support, (c) serialize the pattern list + rebuild from sorted order\n    (deterministic, ~2ms vs ~8ms for unsorted).  Evaluate (c) first as\n    the lowest-risk path.\n  - Prerequisite: 44.2 (profiling infrastructure) to measure actual\n    Scanner::new() cost and confirm this is the dominant startup term.\n  - Scope: new `build.rs`, `engine/scan/mod.rs` (deserialization path).\n  - Gate: Scanner::new() time drops by >=60%.  `cargo test` identical.\n    Binary size increase <500KB.\n\n### 46.2 Split-AC by character class\n- [ ] The monolithic spelling AC mixes pure-CJK patterns (~900 rules,\n      3-byte UTF-8 chars) with mixed ASCII/CJK patterns (~200 rules).\n      A single automaton's state space is the union of both alphabets,\n      inflating the double-array and reducing L1 cache hit rate.\n  - Inspired by the Split-AC algorithm (FPGA literature): partition rules\n    into independent sub-automata that run in parallel over the same input.\n    Each sub-automaton has a smaller alphabet and fewer states, fitting\n    more compactly in cache.\n  - Proposed partition: (a) pure-CJK rules (all chars >= U+2E80) into a\n    charwise DAAC, (b) ASCII-containing rules (case rules, mixed terms)\n    into a bytewise AC.  This mirrors the existing charwise/bytewise\n    fallback split but makes it intentional rather than error-driven.\n  - Caveat: two AC passes over the same input doubles traversal work.\n    Net benefit depends on cache pressure being the dominant cost, not\n    traversal.  Profile first (44.2).  On small documents (<10KB) where\n    the full AC fits in L2 anyway, splitting may hurt.\n  - Alternative: partition by pattern length (short 2-char patterns vs\n    long 4+ char patterns).  Short patterns dominate state fan-out;\n    isolating them reduces the long-pattern automaton's size.\n  - Scope: `engine/scan/mod.rs` (Scanner::new, dual-AC dispatch),\n    `engine/scan/spelling.rs` (merge results from both ACs).\n  - Gate: L1-dcache-load-misses reduction measurable via `perf stat`.\n    Identical scan output.  No regression on documents <10KB.\n\n### 46.3 SIMD structural prefilter for exclusion zone detection\n- [ ] Markdown exclusion zone detection (code fences, inline code, HTML\n      tags) currently uses pulldown-cmark, which parses the full AST.\n      For documents where exclusion zones are sparse, a lightweight SIMD\n      prefilter can skip pulldown-cmark entirely on clean segments.\n  - The Teddy algorithm (Hyperscan lineage, integrated in BurntSushi's\n    aho-corasick crate) is optimal for small pattern sets (<100 patterns)\n    and scans at 10+ GB/s using PSHUFB vector shuffles.  Markdown\n    structural tokens (```, ~~~, `<`, `<!--`, `[`, `![`) are exactly\n    the kind of small, fixed set Teddy excels at.\n  - Approach: run a Teddy-backed AC over the raw input to locate\n    structural token positions.  If none found, bypass pulldown-cmark\n    and scan the entire document as plain text.  If found, invoke\n    pulldown-cmark only on the relevant regions.\n  - Caveat: pulldown-cmark is ~4.6% of scan CPU (45.3 measurement).\n    SIMD prefilter adds code complexity for marginal gain on typical\n    Markdown documents.  Most valuable for plain-text inputs mistyped\n    as Markdown, where pulldown-cmark does unnecessary work.\n  - Prerequisite: 44.2 (profiling) to confirm pulldown-cmark cost on\n    real workloads.  45.3 (exclusion zone optimization) is the broader\n    item; this is a specific SIMD tactic within that scope.\n  - Scope: `engine/markdown.rs` (prefilter gate), `engine/excluded.rs`.\n  - Gate: plain-text-as-markdown scan time reduced.  No regression on\n    actual Markdown documents.  Identical exclusion zones.",
